Every entry on a drive the guest serves is readable and writable.

Modes and flags come off the disk and are whatever wrote them: WSL's $LXMOD on
NTFS, a Linux umask, a Windows read-only attribute, a 0700 folder copied from a
Mac, an immutable or append-only flag. They refused twice: the client judged
the mode it was handed, and a server acting as anyone but root was judged
against the mode stored.

inode_permission() keeps only the refusals that protect data: a read-only
mount, a filesystem's own EROFS (a read-only btrfs snapshot) and EIO. Ownership,
the sticky bit and immutable and append-only flags refuse nothing, except on
the kernel's internal pseudo filesystems, which rely on the flag. ext4 still
treats either flag on a symlink as corruption. vfs_getattr_nosec(), where stat,
statx and nfsd read attributes for every filesystem including FUSE, adds read
and write for everyone, and search on folders. Nothing is written to the disk.

